<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>MartenStudio</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>MartenStudio</RootNamespace>
    <PackageId>MartenStudio</PackageId>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageTags>marten;martendb;postgresql;event-sourcing;document-database;admin;dashboard;ui;blazor;aspnetcore</PackageTags>
    <Description>An embeddable Blazor Server admin UI for MartenDB. Register it with AddMartenStudio() and mount it with MapMartenStudio() inside your own ASP.NET Core application; it runs against that application's own configured IDocumentStore. Browse document collections and JSON, inspect and steer event streams, projections and the async daemon, run guarded queries and manage schema. Every mutating operation is an individual capability flag and the studio refuses to start without an explicit authorization decision.</Description>
    <EnablePackageValidation>true</EnablePackageValidation>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Not trimmable, said out loud rather than left to the default. Blazor Server is a reflective
    framework by construction: a component's [Parameter] properties are set by name from the render
    tree, and the document viewer deserializes into types discovered from IDocumentType.DocumentType
    at run time. Marking this IsTrimmable would tell a trimmer it may cut into a graph nothing
    statically references. README.md says the same for anyone reading on nuget.org.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsTrimmable>false</IsTrimmable>
    <IsAotCompatible>false</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="../../README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Blazor Server, SignalR, authorization and the endpoint routing the studio mounts into all
         come from the shared framework, so the package pins no ASP.NET Core patch on its host. -->
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
    <PackageReference Include="Marten" />
    <!--
      blazor.web.js, for the host.

      As of .NET 10 that file is not in the shared framework and not embedded in any ASP.NET Core
      assembly (checked: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server/Web/Endpoints carry no manifest
      resources). It comes from this pack, which the SDK resolves only for a project whose
      `RequiresAspNetWebAssets` is true - and the Web SDK defaults that to true only for a project that
      has `.razor` Content items of its own (`ResolveRequiredWebAssets` in
      Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web.ProjectSystem.targets). A host that adds Marten Studio and writes no Blazor
      components has none, so `GET /_framework/blazor.web.js` was a 404, the studio prerendered, never
      became interactive, and nothing said a word.

      The package cannot set the property on the consumer's behalf: it is a restore-time decision and
      NuGet deliberately excludes package-provided .props/.targets from restore evaluation
      (ExcludeRestorePackageImports) - measured, not assumed. A plain PackageReference is what does
      reach the consumer: this pack's buildTransitive targets run in any Web SDK executable that
      references the studio, directly or transitively, and define `_framework/blazor.web.js` as that
      project's OWN static web asset. So the script is served by the host's own
      MapStaticAssets()/UseStaticFiles(), the studio's mapped {Path}/_framework/blazor.web.js endpoint
      forwards to it, and nothing here ships a copy of a Microsoft script that would go stale one patch
      after the package was built. A floor rather than an exact pin, like Marten: a host whose own SDK
      already resolves this pack (any project with .razor files of its own) keeps its version, because
      NuGet takes the higher of the two.

      No lib/, no ref/, no runtime assembly - three .js files and one MSBuild target - so this is not
      an ASP.NET Core patch pinned onto the host (D3). Verified 2026-09-14 with an API-only host that
      has no .razor file, does not set RequiresAspNetWebAssets and references only the MartenStudio
      nupkg: /_framework/blazor.web.js 200, /marten/_framework/blazor.web.js 200, negotiate 200.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Internal.Assets" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="MartenStudio.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="MartenStudio.Integration.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
